---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About - Batuhan Ozturk" description="AI & RevOps Architect building the data infrastructure that lets AI results compound instead of evaporating after a demo.">
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <header class="text-center mb-12">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-2">Batuhan Öztürk</h1>
      <p class="text-accent font-medium">AI & RevOps Architect</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-neutral dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
      <div class="text-lg leading-relaxed mb-8">
        <p class="mb-6">
          Three-time national champion volleyball player. M.A. in Sport and Movement Science, Universität Hamburg. Currently Senior Digital Automation Manager at <a href="https://ai-group.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:underline">AI.GROUP</a>.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-6">
          That combination of athletic discipline, sociological training, and hands-on AI architecture experience underlies a specific view of where AI investment goes wrong today. Most of it goes toward models, tools, and frameworks — the visible, easy-to-market layer. The more consistent constraint sits underneath: data. Without a structured foundation, AI output is inconsistent, and value tends to disappear once a session ends rather than compounding over time. A good result in one conversation rarely becomes a repeatable business asset, because nothing was built to carry it forward.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-6">
          Closing that gap is the actual work: data architecture and pipelines that connect business systems to AI in a durable, auditable way, so results persist and scale instead of resetting with every new use case. At AI.GROUP, this spans automation and AI initiatives across the group's portfolio companies, from early-stage diagnostics to full production systems. A representative example is a project built for <a href="https://ai-fund.vc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-accent hover:underline">AI.FUND</a> — an autonomous system that manages the deal flow pipeline end to end, ingesting and analyzing pitch decks, researching the companies behind them, and generating investment notes without manual handoffs between steps.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-6">
          That kind of system only holds up if the underlying data problem is understood correctly, which is where the background outside of engineering matters. Competitive athletics — a national volleyball career spanning three championship titles — built the discipline and systemized execution that carries over directly into how these systems get designed: repeatable, tested under pressure, not left to chance. The sociology background, centered on a Master's thesis on athlete self-presentation and organizational communication, explains the other half of the problem: structured data collection tends to fail for human and organizational reasons as often as technical ones, and fixing it requires reading both. AI and automation architecture is the layer that ties the two together into something that actually ships.
        </p>

        <p class="mb-8">
          My view is that AI results only count once they hold up past the first demo, not just inside it. This site is where I document that view: how the data layer gets built, what tends to break it, and what a working pipeline looks like in practice.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="border-t border-border pt-12 pb-8">
        <p class="mb-8 text-center">
          If you'd like to get to know my background or exchange thoughts on these topics, you can find me here.
        </p>
